  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 212 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Mostly dry and seasonable conditions are expected to \ncontinue across the region through the remainder of the week, with a \nfew chances for showers possible. High temperatures will generally be \nin the 70s to low 80s the next several days. Another round of patchy \nfog will be possible tonight into tomorrow morning with high \npressure overhead, but moisture will be limited which will influence \nhow much fog develops overnight. A decaying cold front will move \nthrough the region tomorrow, bringing chances for scattered showers \nand possible embedded thunderstorms, particularly across northern \nNew York. Showers and any thunderstorms that do develop look to be \nrather unimpactful, with limited dynamics and instability to promote \nany stronger thunderstorms. Shower chances may linger into the \nevening, especially across the higher terrain before drier \nconditions return to the region. Some showers cannot be entirely \nruled out on Thursday as a weak area of low pressure moving across \nsouthern New England, but chances are fairly low and dry conditions \nare expected to prevail across most of the area, with dry and \nseasonable conditions continuing into Friday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A deep 500 millibar trough is expected to dig into the \nGreat Lakes region Saturday night, leading to widespread showers \noverspreading our area into Sunday. Expect showers will tend to be \norographically enhanced with this setup, but thankfully there are \nare no signals for noteworthy moisture or heat to advect northward, \nwhich limits potential for impactful/hazardous weather. As such, the \nlatest 48 hour rainfall probabilities of exceeding 1.00\" of rain are \nonly in the 15 - 40% range with 2.00\" probabilities below 10%. \nGreatest chances of the higher rainfall amounts appear primarily in \nthe western foothills of the Adirondacks and westward into the St. \nLawrence Valley. Given the aforementioned meridional winds and \nlikely deep upper level low, would expect a fairly long duration \nevent with at least periods of showers. Generally this system should \nbring plenty of cloud cover and cool air from the northwest by \nMonday, such that daytime temperatures will trend temporarily below \nnormal before recovering towards midweek following the departure of \nthe trough.",
